---
import StudioLayout from '../../layouts/StudioLayout.astro';
import '../../styles/thermometry.css';
---
<StudioLayout title="Thermometry - Physics with Harshith" chapterChip="Physics · Thermometry">

  <!-- =========== HOME =========== -->
  <section id="home" class="screen active">
    <div class="hero">
      <h1>Thermo<span class="accent">metry</span></h1>
      <p>Nothing in the world can feel temperature directly. So every thermometer measures something else - a length, a pressure, a resistance - and turns that into a temperature. Six stations to build a scale, and a seventh that follows the gas thermometer down to absolute zero.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">

      <div class="tile" onclick="go('why')">
        <div class="tile-num">01</div>
        <div class="tile-icon ic-therm"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M14 14.76V4.5a2.5 2.5 0 0 0-5 0v10.26a4.5 4.5 0 1 0 5 0z"/><path d="M11.5 8h2.5M11.5 11h2.5"/></svg></div>
        <h3>How Does Mercury Know?</h3>
        <p>It doesn't. Heat something up, watch a property change, and meet the word that runs this whole chapter - thermometric property.</p>
        <span class="tile-go">Start ›</span>
      </div>

      <div class="tile" onclick="go('calibrate')">
        <div class="tile-num">02</div>
        <div class="tile-icon ic-cal"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 20h16"/><path d="M6 20V8M10 20V4M14 20V8M18 20V4"/></svg></div>
        <h3>Calibrating Celsius</h3>
        <p>Ice point, steam point, one hundred equal parts. Then the catch: mercury and alcohol still disagree in between.</p>
        <span class="tile-go">Start ›</span>
      </div>

      <div class="tile" onclick="go('master')">
        <div class="tile-num">03</div>
        <div class="tile-icon ic-formula"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 12h16"/><path d="M7 7l4 5-4 5"/><path d="M15 7h4M15 17h4"/></svg></div>
        <h3>The Master Formula</h3>
        <p>Drag the property and watch the temperature move by the same fraction. One formula that becomes p, R or l just by changing the letter.</p>
        <span class="tile-go">Start ›</span>
      </div>

      <div class="tile" onclick="go('types')">
        <div class="tile-num">04</div>
        <div class="tile-icon ic-range"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 6h11M3 12h17M3 18h8"/><path d="M18 3l3 3-3 3"/></svg></div>
        <h3>Thermometers &amp; Ranges</h3>
        <p>Five thermometers on one temperature line. Tap a bar to see what it measures and why its range stops where it stops.</p>
        <span class="tile-go">Start ›</span>
      </div>

      <div class="tile" onclick="go('faulty')">
        <div class="tile-num">05</div>
        <div class="tile-icon ic-faulty"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M10.29 3.86L1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0z"/><path d="M12 9v4M12 17h.01"/></svg></div>
        <h3>Faulty Thermometers</h3>
        <p>The JEE favourite. Break the ice point and the steam point, then see why just subtracting the error is wrong.</p>
        <span class="tile-go">Start ›</span>
      </div>

      <div class="tile" onclick="go('homework')">
        <div class="tile-num">06</div>
        <div class="tile-icon ic-hw"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M14 2H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v16a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h12a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z"/><path d="M14 2v6h6M9 15l2 2 4-4"/></svg></div>
        <h3>Homework &amp; Rapid Fire</h3>
        <p>Five problems with full solutions on tap, plus a quick recap round to finish the lecture.</p>
        <span class="tile-go">Start ›</span>
      </div>

      <div class="tile" onclick="location.href='/thermometry/lecture-2'">
        <div class="tile-num">07</div>
        <div class="tile-icon ic-gasth"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="7" cy="17" r="4"/><path d="M7 13V5M7 5h10M17 5v9M17 14a3 3 0 0 0 0 6"/></svg></div>
        <h3>The Absolute Scale</h3>
        <p>The gas thermometer that does not care what is inside it, the extrapolation to -273.15 &deg;C, and the Kelvin scale. All animations.</p>
        <span class="tile-go">Start &rsaquo;</span>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- =========== 1 · HOW DOES MERCURY KNOW? =========== -->
  <section id="why" class="screen">
    <div class="scr-head">
      <button class="back-btn" onclick="go('home')">‹ Back</button>
      <div>
        <div class="scr-title">How Does Mercury Know?</div>
        <div class="scr-sub">It doesn't. Every thermometer measures something else, then converts it.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="th-controls">
      <div class="th-ctrl">
        <label>Temperature of the body <span class="th-val" id="thWhyTVal">25 °C</span></label>
        <input type="range" id="thWhyT" min="-80" max="360" step="1" value="25">
      </div>
      <div class="th-btns" id="thWhyProps">
        <button class="th-btn on" data-prop="liquid">Liquid column</button>
        <button class="th-btn" data-prop="gas">Gas pressure</button>
        <button class="th-btn" data-prop="res">Resistance</button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="th-canvas" id="thWhyCanvas"></div>

    <div class="th-readouts">
      <div class="th-readout"><small>Temperature t</small><div class="th-ro" id="thWhyRoT">25 °C</div></div>
      <div class="th-readout"><small id="thWhyRoXlab">Property X</small><div class="th-ro" id="thWhyRoX">-</div></div>
      <div class="th-readout"><small>Working range</small><div class="th-ro small" id="thWhyRoRange">-</div></div>
      <div class="th-readout"><small>Status</small><div class="th-ro small" id="thWhyRoStat">-</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="th-keybox">
      <b>Mercury does not know what temperature is.</b> All it can do is expand when it gets hot. Drag the slider past the ends of the green band and watch the graph stop being a straight line - that is a thermometer going out of range.
    </div>

    <div class="th-notes">
      <div class="th-notes-head">Copy this into your notes</div>
      <div class="th-notes-body">
        <h4>Thermometric property</h4>
        <div class="th-nbox">
          <b>Nothing can touch a temperature.</b> So pick something that changes when the temperature changes, measure THAT, and convert it. What you measure is the <b>thermometric property</b>.
        </div>
        <div class="th-props">
          <div class="th-prop"><div class="th-pn">01</div><div class="th-pt">Volume of a liquid</div><div class="th-pd">mercury / alcohol thermometer</div></div>
          <div class="th-prop"><div class="th-pn">02</div><div class="th-pt">Length of a solid</div><div class="th-pd">rod thermometers</div></div>
          <div class="th-prop"><div class="th-pn">03</div><div class="th-pt">Pressure of a gas at constant volume</div><div class="th-pd">constant volume gas thermometer</div></div>
          <div class="th-prop"><div class="th-pn">04</div><div class="th-pt">Volume of a gas at constant pressure</div><div class="th-pd">constant pressure gas thermometer</div></div>
          <div class="th-prop"><div class="th-pn">05</div><div class="th-pt">Electrical resistance of a conductor</div><div class="th-pd">platinum resistance thermometer</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="th-nbox">
          <b>It must change in a STRAIGHT LINE with temperature, over the range you use it in.</b> Where the line bends, the thermometer stops working - that is why every thermometer comes with a stated <b>range</b>.
        </div>
        <div class="th-eg" role="button" tabindex="0">
          <span class="th-eglab">Think about it</span>
          A mercury thermometer is useless at -100 °C. The glass is fine and the numbers are fine - so what has gone wrong?
          <span class="th-eghint">Tap to reveal the answer</span>
          <div class="th-egans">
            Mercury freezes at -39 °C. A solid does not expand when you warm it a little, so the property has stopped changing - there is nothing left to measure. Below -39 °C use alcohol (it freezes only at -115 °C) or a gas thermometer.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </section>

  <!-- =========== 2 · CALIBRATING CELSIUS =========== -->
  <section id="calibrate" class="screen">
    <div class="scr-head">
      <button class="back-btn" onclick="go('home')">‹ Back</button>
      <div>
        <div class="scr-title">Calibrating the Celsius Scale</div>
        <div class="scr-sub">Two fixed points, one hundred parts - and one problem left over.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="th-chips" id="thCalTabs">
      <button class="th-chip active" data-pane="thCalA">1 · Build the scale</button>
      <button class="th-chip" data-pane="thCalB">2 · Two thermometers disagree</button>
    </div>

    <!-- ── build the scale ── -->
    <div class="th-pane active" id="thCalA">
      <div class="th-controls">
        <div class="th-btns">
          <button class="th-btn primary" id="thCalS1">1 · Dip in melting ice</button>
          <button class="th-btn" id="thCalS2">2 · Hold in steam</button>
          <button class="th-btn" id="thCalS3">3 · Divide into 100</button>
          <button class="th-btn" id="thCalReset">↺ Start over</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="th-canvas" id="thCalCanvas"></div>

      <div class="th-keybox">
        <b>Nobody discovered 0 and 100 - we chose them.</b> We picked ice and steam because anyone, anywhere, can set them up and get the same temperature every time. We picked 100 parts because it is easy to work with. The mercury just sits there and expands.
      </div>

      <div class="th-notes">
        <div class="th-notes-head">Copy this into your notes</div>
        <div class="th-notes-body">
          <h4>Calibrating the Celsius scale</h4>
          <div class="th-nstep"><b>Step 1</b> · Melting ice. Mark where the mercury stops - call it <b>0 °C</b>, the <b>ice point</b>.</div>
          <div class="th-nstep"><b>Step 2</b> · Steam above boiling water, at normal air pressure. Mark it <b>100 °C</b>, the <b>steam point</b>.</div>
          <div class="th-nstep"><b>Step 3</b> · Cut the gap into <b>100 equal parts</b>. Each part is 1 °C - hence the old name centigrade.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>

    <!-- ── the disagreement ── -->
    <div class="th-pane" id="thCalB">
      <div class="th-controls">
        <div class="th-ctrl">
          <label>True temperature of the bath <span class="th-val" id="thCmpTVal">40 °C</span></label>
          <input type="range" id="thCmpT" min="0" max="100" step="1" value="40">
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="th-canvas" id="thCmpCanvas"></div>

      <div class="th-readouts">
        <div class="th-readout"><small>Mercury reads</small><div class="th-ro" id="thCmpHg">-</div></div>
        <div class="th-readout"><small>Alcohol reads</small><div class="th-ro" id="thCmpAl">-</div></div>
        <div class="th-readout"><small>Disagreement</small><div class="th-ro bad" id="thCmpGap">-</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="th-keybox">
        <b>Slide to 0 and to 100:</b> the gap becomes exactly zero, because those are the two points we fixed by hand. Everywhere in between, the answer depends on what is inside the tube. Nothing is broken here - this is built into the way we defined the scale.
      </div>

      <div class="th-notes">
        <div class="th-notes-head">Copy this into your notes</div>
        <div class="th-notes-body">
          <h4>The problem left over</h4>
          <div class="th-nbox">
            <b>If your scale is built on one liquid, the answer depends on that liquid.</b><br>
            Mercury and alcohol agree at 0 and 100 because we forced them to. In between they need not - the two liquids do not expand in quite the same way.
          </div>
          <p>The gas thermometer fixes this - that is the <b>Absolute Scale</b> activity, and following its graph leads straight to the coldest temperature that can exist.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- =========== 3 · THE MASTER FORMULA =========== -->
  <section id="master" class="screen">
    <div class="scr-head">
      <button class="back-btn" onclick="go('home')">‹ Back</button>
      <div>
        <div class="scr-title">The Master Formula</div>
        <div class="scr-sub">How far along the property has gone is how far along the temperature has gone.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="th-controls">
      <div class="th-btns" id="thMasterProps">
        <button class="th-btn on" data-prop="R">Resistance R (Ω)</button>
        <button class="th-btn" data-prop="p">Pressure p (cm Hg)</button>
        <button class="th-btn" data-prop="l">Length l (cm)</button>
        <button class="th-btn" data-prop="X">Unknown X (units)</button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="th-controls">
      <div class="th-ctrl">
        <label><span id="thMX0Lab">X₀ at the ice point</span> <span class="th-val" id="thMX0Val">-</span></label>
        <input type="range" id="thMX0" min="0" max="100" step="1" value="20">
      </div>
      <div class="th-ctrl">
        <label><span id="thMX100Lab">X₁₀₀ at the steam point</span> <span class="th-val" id="thMX100Val">-</span></label>
        <input type="range" id="thMX100" min="0" max="100" step="1" value="80">
      </div>
      <div class="th-ctrl">
        <label><span id="thMXtLab">Xₜ measured now</span> <span class="th-val" id="thMXtVal">-</span></label>
        <input type="range" id="thMXt" min="0" max="100" step="1" value="50">
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="th-canvas" id="thMasterCanvas"></div>

    <div class="th-work">
      <div class="th-work-lab">Substituting straight into the master formula</div>
      <div class="th-work-line" id="thMLine1"></div>
      <div class="th-work-line" id="thMLine2"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="th-keybox">
      <b>Watch the two shaded bars.</b> The blue bar shows how far the property has moved; the orange bar shows how far the temperature has moved. They are always exactly the same length - and that is the formula, drawn as a picture. Notice the temperature can also go below 0 or above 100: the formula still works there, as long as the property keeps changing in a straight line.
    </div>

    <div class="th-notes">
      <div class="th-notes-head">Copy this into your notes</div>
      <div class="th-notes-body">
        <h4>The master formula</h4>
        <div class="th-nf">X<sub>0</sub> = value at 0 °C &nbsp;·&nbsp; X<sub>100</sub> = value at 100 °C &nbsp;·&nbsp; X<sub>t</sub> = value at the unknown t</div>
        <div class="th-nf" style="font-size:26px;">t = [ (X<sub>t</sub> − X<sub>0</sub>) / (X<sub>100</sub> − X<sub>0</sub>) ] × 100 °C</div>
        <div class="th-nbox">
          Say it out loud: <b>how far along the property has gone is how far along the temperature has gone.</b> (Proper name: <b>linear interpolation</b>.)
        </div>
        <h4>One formula, three letters</h4>
        <div class="th-nf">Gas &nbsp; X → p &nbsp;·&nbsp; Platinum resistance &nbsp; X → R &nbsp;·&nbsp; Mercury &nbsp; X → l</div>
        <div class="th-nbox">
          Your textbook prints these as three separate formulas. They are one formula wearing three letters - so a property you have never seen before is still doable.
        </div>
        <div class="th-eg" role="button" tabindex="0">
          <span class="th-eglab">Worked example</span>
          A platinum resistance thermometer reads 5.0 Ω at the ice point, 5.4 Ω at the steam point, and 5.3 Ω in a hot liquid. Find the temperature of the liquid.
          <span class="th-eghint">Tap to reveal the answer</span>
          <div class="th-egans">
            t = [(5.3 − 5.0) / (5.4 − 5.0)] × 100 = (0.3 / 0.4) × 100 = <b>75 °C</b>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </section>

  <!-- =========== 4 · THERMOMETERS & RANGES =========== -->
  <section id="types" class="screen">
    <div class="scr-head">
      <button class="back-btn" onclick="go('home')">‹ Back</button>
      <div>
        <div class="scr-title">Thermometers &amp; Their Ranges</div>
        <div class="scr-sub">Five thermometers on one temperature line - tap a bar.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="th-canvas" id="thTypesCanvas"></div>

    <div class="th-work">
      <div class="th-work-lab" id="thTypeLab">Tap a bar above</div>
      <div class="th-work-line" id="thTypeInfo" style="font-size:19px;line-height:1.6;">Each bar is one thermometer, drawn on a real temperature line. Tap any of them to see what it measures and the reason its range stops where it stops.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="th-keybox">
      <b>A range is never a random number.</b> Every end point on that chart is a bit of physics: a liquid freezing, a metal being spoiled, a gas turning liquid, or radiation getting too faint to catch.
    </div>

    <div class="th-notes">
      <div class="th-notes-head">Copy this into your notes</div>
      <div class="th-notes-body">
        <h4>Thermometer types and ranges</h4>
        <div class="th-nf">Mercury &nbsp;·&nbsp; length of Hg column &nbsp;·&nbsp; −30 to 300 °C</div>
        <div class="th-nf">Constant volume gas &nbsp;·&nbsp; pressure of gas &nbsp;·&nbsp; −268 to 1500 °C</div>
        <div class="th-nf">Platinum resistance &nbsp;·&nbsp; electrical resistance &nbsp;·&nbsp; −200 to 1200 °C</div>
        <div class="th-nf">Thermocouple &nbsp;·&nbsp; thermo-emf &nbsp;·&nbsp; −200 to 1600 °C</div>
        <div class="th-nf">Radiation pyrometer &nbsp;·&nbsp; emitted radiation &nbsp;·&nbsp; 800 °C to no upper limit</div>
        <div class="th-nbox">
          Mercury <b>freezes at −39 °C</b>. The pyrometer <b>never touches</b> the body, so it has no upper limit, but it fails below 800 °C. The <b>gas thermometer</b> reaches the lowest temperatures and is the standard the others are checked against.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </section>

  <!-- =========== 5 · FAULTY THERMOMETERS =========== -->
  <section id="faulty" class="screen">
    <div class="scr-head">
      <button class="back-btn" onclick="go('home')">‹ Back</button>
      <div>
        <div class="scr-title">Faulty Thermometers</div>
        <div class="scr-sub">The question type JEE asks most often - so give it real time.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="th-controls">
      <div class="th-ctrl">
        <label>Faulty ice point <span class="th-val" id="thFIceVal">5</span></label>
        <input type="range" id="thFIce" min="-15" max="20" step="1" value="5">
      </div>
      <div class="th-ctrl">
        <label>Faulty steam point <span class="th-val" id="thFStmVal">99</span></label>
        <input type="range" id="thFStm" min="80" max="120" step="1" value="99">
      </div>
      <div class="th-ctrl">
        <label>Faulty thermometer reads <span class="th-val" id="thFRdVal">52</span></label>
        <input type="range" id="thFRd" min="-15" max="120" step="1" value="52">
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="th-canvas" id="thFaultyCanvas"></div>

    <div class="th-readouts">
      <div class="th-readout"><small>Correct - proportion</small><div class="th-ro good" id="thFTrue">-</div></div>
      <div class="th-readout"><small>Wrong - just subtract</small><div class="th-ro bad" id="thFNaive">-</div></div>
      <div class="th-readout"><small>Error the shortcut makes</small><div class="th-ro small" id="thFErr">-</div></div>
      <div class="th-readout"><small>In Fahrenheit</small><div class="th-ro small" id="thFF">-</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="th-work">
      <div class="th-work-lab">Live substitution</div>
      <div class="th-work-line" id="thFLine"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="th-keybox">
      <b>Drag the reading and watch the red number.</b> The "just subtract the error" answer only matches the truth by luck. Drag the steam point away from 100 and the two answers pull further and further apart - because now every division is the wrong size too.
    </div>

    <div class="th-notes">
      <div class="th-notes-head">Copy this into your notes</div>
      <div class="th-notes-body">
        <h4>Faulty thermometers</h4>
        <div class="th-nbox">
          The marks are still 100 equal parts, so <b>the scale is still a straight line</b> - just wrong at both ends. Use the <b>faulty readings as your two fixed points</b>.
        </div>
        <div class="th-nf">true temp = [ (faulty reading − faulty ice point) / (faulty steam point − faulty ice point) ] × 100</div>
        <div class="th-warn">
          <b>DON'T just add or subtract the error.</b> Two things are wrong at once: the <b>zero</b> is misplaced AND <b>each division is the wrong size</b>. Only the proportion fixes both.
        </div>
        <div class="th-eg" role="button" tabindex="0">
          <span class="th-eglab">Example 1</span>
          Reads 5 °C in melting ice and 99 °C in steam. Find the correct temperature in °F when it reads 52 °C.
          <span class="th-eghint">Tap to reveal the answer</span>
          <div class="th-egans">
            t = [(52 − 5) / (99 − 5)] × 100 = (47 / 94) × 100 = 50 °C, then T<sub>F</sub> = (9/5)(50) + 32 = <b>122 °F</b>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="th-eg" role="button" tabindex="0">
          <span class="th-eglab">Example 2 - do it together</span>
          Reads −2 °C in ice and 103 °C in steam, and 58 °C in a liquid. Find the true temperature.
          <span class="th-eghint">Tap to reveal the answer</span>
          <div class="th-egans">
            t = [(58 − (−2)) / (103 − (−2))] × 100 = (60 / 105) × 100 = <b>57.14 °C</b>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </section>

  <!-- =========== 6 · HOMEWORK & RAPID FIRE =========== -->
  <section id="homework" class="screen">
    <div class="scr-head">
      <button class="back-btn" onclick="go('home')">‹ Back</button>
      <div>
        <div class="scr-title">Homework &amp; Rapid Fire</div>
        <div class="scr-sub">Solve first, then tap to check.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="th-chips" id="thHwTabs">
      <button class="th-chip active" data-pane="thHwA">Homework · 5 questions</button>
      <button class="th-chip" data-pane="thHwB">Rapid fire recap</button>
    </div>

    <div class="th-pane active" id="thHwA">
      <div class="th-hw" role="button" tabindex="0">
        <div class="th-hw-q"><span class="th-hw-n">1</span>A resistance thermometer reads 2.5 Ω at 0 °C and 3.5 Ω at 100 °C. What is the temperature when the resistance is 3.1 Ω?</div>
        <div class="th-hw-sol">
          Master formula with X → R:<br>
          t = [(3.1 − 2.5) / (3.5 − 2.5)] × 100 = (0.6 / 1.0) × 100 = <b>60 °C</b>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="th-hw" role="button" tabindex="0">
        <div class="th-hw-q"><span class="th-hw-n">2</span>A constant volume gas thermometer records 80 cm of Hg at the ice point and 110 cm of Hg at the steam point. Find the temperature when the pressure reads 95 cm of Hg.</div>
        <div class="th-hw-sol">
          Master formula with X → p:<br>
          t = [(95 − 80) / (110 − 80)] × 100 = (15 / 30) × 100 = <b>50 °C</b><br>
          The pressure is exactly halfway between its two fixed values, so the temperature is exactly halfway too. That is the straight line doing its job.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="th-hw" role="button" tabindex="0">
        <div class="th-hw-q"><span class="th-hw-n">3</span>A faulty thermometer reads 1 °C in melting ice and 97 °C in steam. What does it read when the TRUE temperature is 40 °C? <i>(Careful - here you know the answer and have to find the reading.)</i></div>
        <div class="th-hw-sol">
          Same equation, different unknown. Let the faulty reading be F:<br>
          40 = [(F − 1) / (97 − 1)] × 100 &nbsp;→&nbsp; F − 1 = (40 / 100) × 96 = 38.4<br>
          F = 38.4 + 1 = <b>39.4 °C on the faulty scale</b><br>
          <b>Note:</b> this is not a new method - it is the same proportion, just solved for a different letter.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="th-hw" role="button" tabindex="0">
        <div class="th-hw-q"><span class="th-hw-n">4</span>A thermometer has the ice point marked 20 and the steam point marked 170, in arbitrary units. Find the temperature in °C when it reads 95 units.</div>
        <div class="th-hw-sol">
          The property is now just "units on this scale" - the formula does not care:<br>
          t = [(95 − 20) / (170 − 20)] × 100 = (75 / 150) × 100 = <b>50 °C</b>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="th-hw" role="button" tabindex="0">
        <div class="th-hw-q"><span class="th-hw-n">5</span><i>Conceptual.</i> Why can a mercury thermometer not measure the temperature of liquid nitrogen (−196 °C)? Which thermometer would you use instead, and why?</div>
        <div class="th-hw-sol">
          Mercury <b>freezes at −39 °C</b>. At −196 °C it is a solid lump, so the length of the column no longer changes with temperature - there is nothing left to measure.<br>
          Use a <b>constant volume gas thermometer</b> (works down to about −268 °C) or a <b>platinum resistance thermometer</b> (down to about −200 °C). Gas pressure and electrical resistance are still changing nicely at that temperature.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

